#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129982} :activity/id #uuid "427e5f35-5438-583c-9454-df8e3ae0c517" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050593 :topic/id #uuid "56f267b9-1121-55c5-a6c5-4c7ce248197a" :topic/math-academy-id 2019} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130356 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **block diagonalizable** if it is similar to a block diagonal matrix $B.$ That is, there exists a block diagonal matrix $B$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis block diagonalizable because it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the matrix $A$ has eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1 = 2},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2=3-2\\textrm i},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3=3+2\\textrm i}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda_3}.$\nThe eigenvectors of ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3},$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, notice that $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}_3}.$\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1},$ and the second and third columns correspond to the real and the imaginary parts of ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8edf8405-33c8-5922-8946-6f87b2881781" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3597 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2079dce9-f014-5601-a83c-150fae42ef41" :step/math-academy-id 20173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128948 :question/id #uuid "1cc03cef-e717-5ac1-ab9d-75508f3aa056" :question/math-academy-id "e-5533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d4c1a7b-2341-5b8c-8112-0290f8eda65f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048195}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269078}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "6045c7fa-0f99-5ffd-9594-e626c92e4020" :step/math-academy-id 20174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130357 :tutorial/content "We have the following theorem regarding the block diagonalization of a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix:\n\n> *If $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A=PBP^{-1},\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where the matrices $B$ and $P$ are given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n\nIn other words, if $A$ is a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue and two complex eigenvalues, then $A$ is block diagonalizable.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The block of $B$ constructed from the real and imaginary parts of $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We can write the blocks of $B$ in either order. However, the order of the columns of $P$ *must* correspond to the order of the blocks." :tutorial/id #uuid "56c5960d-6197-586e-850e-8f1a23e2289a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3598 :tutorial/title "The Block Diagonalization Theorem for a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9ac92ee1-74aa-5b6d-a07e-d4fc3f68f30b" :step/math-academy-id 20175 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128949 :question/id #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" :question/math-academy-id "e-5534"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388437}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5d6954b7-9d4b-57e8-aee6-999d162199be" :step/math-academy-id 20176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129986}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130358 :tutorial/content "We can find a block diagonalization for any real $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A.$ This means that for any square matrix $A,$ we can construct a block diagonal matrix $B$ and invertible matrix $P$ such that \n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere the blocks of $B$ are either:\n\n- $1\\!\\times\\! 1$ blocks containing the real eigenvalues of $A,$ or\n\n- $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ blocks containing the rotation-scaling matrices corresponding to the complex (not real) eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe columns of $P$ form bases of the eigenspaces for the real eigenvectors, or come in the pairs $\\left(\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{v}), \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{v})\\right)$ for the non-real eigenvectors.\n\nLet's see an example of block diagonalization for higher-dimensional matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "650f30ea-d42a-5fba-bd00-3f269007df84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3599 :tutorial/title "Generalization of Block Diagonalization to NxN Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "695a49a5-60af-5017-b281-1870ceb33c6d" :step/math-academy-id 20177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128950 :question/id #uuid "2b2a7fbc-8911-507f-915c-cf9b50653e5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388396}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d7512dc9-649d-5cd3-992b-9f3d84b178e3" :step/math-academy-id 20178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128978 :question/id #uuid "da515ea7-f4b0-58c5-adab-c826563dd3cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048584}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400723}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"} :step/id #uuid "9a08b734-6db2-5bf9-8aae-178359e681f6" :step/math-academy-id 21723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129983}}] :activity/title "Block Diagonalization of NxN Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129362}]}
